//@host = http://localhost:5000
@host = https://mamelobby-api-fse4a3gbehavfdhd.westcentralus-01.azurewebsites.net/

# Your own public IPv4, which steps 2 and 4 advertise. The server refuses a room
# whose address is not the caller's own, so against a real deployment this has to
# be yours or step 2 answers 400 address_mismatch: get it from https://api.ipify.org
# and expect a dynamic ISP to change it now and then. Against localhost anything
# goes -- the dev settings skip the check. Leave the placeholder here when
# committing: nobody's home address belongs in the repo.
@myIp = 203.0.113.10

# Both sides come from the same machine here, so they differ only by port and the
# answers will say sameSite:true. That is correct, and it is the same conclusion
# the client reaches when two players sit behind one router.
@hostPort = 2080
@clientPort = 41320


### 1. Check server configuration (and Kill Switch)
GET {{host}}/api/v1/config?proto=11&app=1.39.0

### 2. HOST: Create a public room
# @name createRoom
POST {{host}}/api/v1/rooms
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "proto": 11,
  "app": "1.39.0",
  "game": "mslug",
  "mode": 1,
  "delay": 0,
  "plugins": false,
  "public": "{{myIp}}:{{hostPort}}",
  "lan": ["192.168.1.50:2080"],
  "nat": { "sym": false, "pp": true, "upnp": true }
}

### -- Captures the room ID and token created above --
@roomId = {{createRoom.response.body.id}}
@roomToken = {{createRoom.response.body.token}}

### 3. CLIENT: View the room board (You will see the room created above)
GET {{host}}/api/v1/rooms?proto=11

### 4. CLIENT: Join the room (And send its own IP to the server)
POST {{host}}/api/v1/rooms/{{roomId}}/join
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "proto": 11,
  "app": "1.39.0",
  "public": "{{myIp}}:{{clientPort}}",
  "lan": ["192.168.1.100:2080"],
  "nat": { "sym": false, "pp": true, "upnp": false }
}

### 5. HOST: Send heartbeat (Poll) (And the server hands over the Client's IP)
POST {{host}}/api/v1/rooms/{{roomId}}/poll
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "token": "{{roomToken}}"
}

### 6. HOST: Cancel or delete the room
DELETE {{host}}/api/v1/rooms/{{roomId}}?token={{roomToken}}

### 7. BOTH: Report how the session ended (fire and forget, answers 204 no body)
# natSelf/natPeer mirror the two NATs from steps 2 and 4: this pairing is what
# turns a pile of sessions into "punching works unless a side is symmetric".
# outcome: connected | timeout | rom_missing | build_mismatch | cancelled
# path:    lan | punch | forward | upnp        role: host | client
POST {{host}}/api/v1/telemetry
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "proto": 11,
  "app": "1.39.0",
  "game": "mslug",
  "role": "host",
  "outcome": "connected",
  "natSelf": { "sym": false, "pp": true, "upnp": true },
  "natPeer": { "sym": false, "pp": true, "upnp": false },
  "path": "punch",
  "waitMs": 4200
}

### 8. The failure worth measuring: two symmetric NATs cannot punch at all
POST {{host}}/api/v1/telemetry
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "proto": 11,
  "app": "1.39.0",
  "game": "mslug",
  "role": "client",
  "outcome": "timeout",
  "natSelf": { "sym": true, "pp": false, "upnp": false },
  "natPeer": { "sym": true, "pp": false, "upnp": false },
  "path": "punch",
  "waitMs": 60000
}

### Both land in /home/LogFiles/lobby/telemetry-YYYYMM.log, one line each.